1
00:00:03,776 --> 00:00:05,477
[SPEAKER_01]: OK, back in the motel.

2
00:00:06,698 --> 00:00:10,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Monday night, March 15th, the date of this interview with Red Faber.

3
00:00:10,922 --> 00:00:15,926
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm staying in the Hillside Motel here on the far east end of Chicago.

4
00:00:15,946 --> 00:00:23,792
[SPEAKER_01]: It's really outside of town, just off the Chicago Bypass 294.

5
00:00:26,814 --> 00:00:31,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, as it is obvious from this tape, this was a very difficult interview.

6
00:00:32,839 --> 00:00:41,467
[SPEAKER_01]: In fact, it was hardly worth it except to meet Red Faber and to talk to him and to talk to his wife and see the house and some of his things there.

7
00:00:43,328 --> 00:00:44,349
[SPEAKER_01]: He's a very old man.

8
00:00:44,369 --> 00:00:45,590
[SPEAKER_01]: He's 88 years old.

9
00:00:47,031 --> 00:00:54,538
[SPEAKER_01]: He was dressed, as the picture I took of himself and Mrs. Faber will show, in a bathrobe and pajamas.

10
00:00:55,719 --> 00:00:59,983
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess he hardly ever dresses up because, as she says, it's just too much trouble.

11
00:01:00,757 --> 00:01:06,880
[SPEAKER_01]: and he sleeps late, and sometimes, sometimes, as she said, he sleeps to two or three o'clock in the afternoon.

12
00:01:09,482 --> 00:01:11,143
[SPEAKER_01]: But usually it's in the morning.

13
00:01:11,223 --> 00:01:26,771
[SPEAKER_01]: But he's, as the end of that last, end of the tape interview part reveals, he is to bed early at night, and misses the White Sox games because they're on too late for him, early as they are.

14
00:01:29,623 --> 00:01:36,925
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, let's recapitulate the day's activities of Monday, March 15th.

15
00:01:36,945 --> 00:01:41,046
[SPEAKER_01]: It was a clear day, despite all the weather forecasts of snow.

16
00:01:41,126 --> 00:01:48,628
[SPEAKER_01]: And there were, again, announcements tonight of snow practically all over the state, maybe a couple inches in Chicago, but I don't believe it.

17
00:01:49,668 --> 00:01:51,808
[SPEAKER_01]: Today was a clear day, a lot of sunshine.

18
00:01:53,109 --> 00:01:58,990
[SPEAKER_01]: I left the motel here probably around 25 after 9 this morning.

19
00:02:00,407 --> 00:02:01,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Drove up to 294.

20
00:02:02,928 --> 00:02:08,849
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm about, oh, a half a mile to a mile in on Roosevelt Boulevard from that.

21
00:02:10,729 --> 00:02:14,970
[SPEAKER_01]: Got back on 294 and drove over.

22
00:02:16,331 --> 00:02:17,031
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I didn't either.

23
00:02:17,571 --> 00:02:19,712
[SPEAKER_01]: I took the first road I hit, which wasn't 294.

24
00:02:19,792 --> 00:02:24,793
[SPEAKER_01]: It was just before 294, some road, which took me over through other parts of Hillside.

25
00:02:24,813 --> 00:02:30,074
[SPEAKER_01]: I worked my way back on to Interstate 90, which goes right smack into the heart of Chicago.

26
00:02:31,570 --> 00:02:34,111
[SPEAKER_01]: And I was on that for a half an hour.

27
00:02:34,231 --> 00:02:40,112
[SPEAKER_01]: So that was heavy driving, heavy traffic and speeding traffic.

28
00:02:40,212 --> 00:02:41,473
[SPEAKER_01]: And it was busy, busy.

29
00:02:41,513 --> 00:02:43,033
[SPEAKER_01]: And I hadn't been used to that for a while.

30
00:02:43,053 --> 00:02:50,515
[SPEAKER_01]: So it was, I was on my toes, no drowsiness in that part of the trip, although there has been some drowsiness in the trip up to here.

31
00:02:52,575 --> 00:02:55,536
[SPEAKER_01]: This by the way, is the result of my morning breakfast.

32
00:02:55,696 --> 00:02:59,157
[SPEAKER_01]: I went over to the little restaurant across the,

33
00:03:01,090 --> 00:03:03,732
[SPEAKER_01]: street here, I've forgotten the name, but the Golden West, I believe it is.

34
00:03:04,653 --> 00:03:06,074
[SPEAKER_01]: And I had breakfast there.

35
00:03:06,715 --> 00:03:08,376
[SPEAKER_01]: All kinds of schoolgirls there.

36
00:03:08,396 --> 00:03:11,318
[SPEAKER_01]: It looks like there's a girls high school very nearby.

37
00:03:11,358 --> 00:03:21,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Mount Carmel High School might be nearby, although I don't think they're all girls there, but boy, they're all girls in that restaurant, all in their blue jeans, but rather neat.

38
00:03:21,866 --> 00:03:24,829
[SPEAKER_01]: It was different from the usually crummy high school crowd.

39
00:03:25,169 --> 00:03:28,792
[SPEAKER_01]: I thought it might be some girls' college, but the waitress said, no, I don't know what it is.

40
00:03:29,132 --> 00:03:30,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe I'll find out tomorrow.

41
00:03:31,630 --> 00:03:34,471
[SPEAKER_01]: But on the way out, I paid my check.

42
00:03:34,511 --> 00:03:43,434
[SPEAKER_01]: I asked the owner there, what would be the best way to get into Red Faber's location in 105 South Prospect.

43
00:03:43,494 --> 00:03:51,357
[SPEAKER_01]: And I indicated I had my map with me, and I showed him the way I planned to go, which is back 294 to about 127th Street, then in towards 57 and over on 57 to about 105th Prospect.

44
00:03:51,457 --> 00:03:52,837
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just off 57.

45
00:03:52,877 --> 00:03:53,738
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, no, I'd have too many

46
00:04:01,562 --> 00:04:05,526
[SPEAKER_01]: on that 127th Street, and there'd be too many stops.

47
00:04:05,646 --> 00:04:08,088
[SPEAKER_01]: There was no expressway at all.

48
00:04:08,128 --> 00:04:21,440
[SPEAKER_01]: So he said, go in the Eisenhower Expressway, or 90 as it's called, into just about the middle, or shortly before the middle of town, you hit the Dan Ryan Expressway south, which is 94.

49
00:04:22,381 --> 00:04:27,205
[SPEAKER_01]: And 90 continued, and it also joins 94 coming south from Milwaukee.

50
00:04:28,093 --> 00:04:38,878
[SPEAKER_01]: and this is the road to Indiana and south of town, but take this to about 95th Street, when you veers off into 57, and take 57 over to about 105th and get off.

51
00:04:38,898 --> 00:04:39,698
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's what I did.

52
00:04:40,618 --> 00:04:48,922
[SPEAKER_01]: I got off at 101st or 97th or something, and I had to work my way around a little bit before I got to Red Faber's house.

53
00:04:49,362 --> 00:04:51,623
[SPEAKER_01]: But I got there about a quarter after 10.

54
00:04:52,555 --> 00:04:58,599
[SPEAKER_01]: I wasn't sure it was Red Faber's house because there were a couple of prospects in Chicago, and I wasn't sure this was the right south prospect.

55
00:04:59,500 --> 00:05:11,729
[SPEAKER_01]: So I went up to the—I parked on the street opposite the house and went up to the neighbor's house and knocked on the door, rang the bell, and nobody came.

56
00:05:11,749 --> 00:05:17,873
[SPEAKER_01]: I think there was a beauty parlor there, and finally some kid came, wouldn't open the door, but he yelled through the door what I wanted, so I asked if they

57
00:05:18,620 --> 00:05:22,061
[SPEAKER_01]: house next door was where the old ball player Faber said, yeah, it was.

58
00:05:22,141 --> 00:05:23,802
[SPEAKER_01]: So I knew I was in the right place.

59
00:05:24,362 --> 00:05:27,002
[SPEAKER_01]: So I got back to the car and stayed there about 10 minutes.

60
00:05:27,082 --> 00:05:29,563
[SPEAKER_01]: I had said 1030 was the time I would get there.

61
00:05:29,583 --> 00:05:32,724
[SPEAKER_01]: And I, I knew that he slept late and I didn't want to be too early.

62
00:05:32,764 --> 00:05:34,865
[SPEAKER_01]: So I killed a few minutes just to sitting in the car.

63
00:05:35,785 --> 00:05:38,786
[SPEAKER_01]: About 1030 I went up to the house and it was let in.

64
00:05:39,606 --> 00:05:42,087
[SPEAKER_01]: This was a, uh, I'd say a,

65
00:05:43,252 --> 00:05:44,712
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kind of a middle-class neighborhood.

66
00:05:44,732 --> 00:05:45,713
[SPEAKER_01]: It's rather nice.

67
00:05:45,893 --> 00:05:48,353
[SPEAKER_01]: The homes are spaced a little bit apart.

68
00:05:48,393 --> 00:05:54,835
[SPEAKER_01]: They're rather new homes, shaded yards, rather deep yards.

69
00:05:55,975 --> 00:06:00,916
[SPEAKER_01]: A rather nice residential aspect, although not too far away.

70
00:06:00,936 --> 00:06:03,857
[SPEAKER_01]: There are some rather run-down neighborhoods.

71
00:06:03,937 --> 00:06:06,957
[SPEAKER_01]: I've passed some coming in.

72
00:06:08,598 --> 00:06:10,358
[SPEAKER_01]: There are all-white

73
00:06:10,928 --> 00:06:15,751
[SPEAKER_01]: on that street, but there were blacks in neighboring streets not too far away.

74
00:06:15,791 --> 00:06:21,976
[SPEAKER_01]: But even where I passed through some black communities coming over to Faber's house, even those were well-kept up communities.

75
00:06:22,016 --> 00:06:23,637
[SPEAKER_01]: It wasn't any kind of a ghetto at all.

76
00:06:24,818 --> 00:06:34,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Although, according to the chap at the restaurant this morning, he said the colored communities, he used the word threatening this part of town where Faber lived.

77
00:06:34,564 --> 00:06:37,486
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not in there yet, but they're threatening, the way he put it.

78
00:06:37,506 --> 00:06:38,747
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not quite sure what that meant.

79
00:06:39,178 --> 00:06:43,179
[SPEAKER_01]: But there were a few blacks nearby, but they were in a rather nice area.

80
00:06:43,639 --> 00:06:45,440
[SPEAKER_01]: So it was kind of a nice place.

81
00:06:45,480 --> 00:06:55,142
[SPEAKER_01]: The home was fairly old, probably 50 years old, but it's a rather neat one-story house, beautifully furnished.

82
00:06:56,802 --> 00:07:07,605
[SPEAKER_01]: And I was met at the door by a lady, well, young lady, she's probably my age, gray-haired, very good-looking and healthy and husky and full of life.

83
00:07:08,765 --> 00:07:10,386
[SPEAKER_01]: And she said, I'm Mrs. Faber.

84
00:07:10,586 --> 00:07:17,831
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, she must have been the one I talked to on the phone, because it sounded like her.

85
00:07:17,871 --> 00:07:21,193
[SPEAKER_01]: And then I thought it'd been some housekeepers taking care of Faber.

86
00:07:21,213 --> 00:07:22,814
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't think his wife would still be around.

87
00:07:22,874 --> 00:07:23,534
[SPEAKER_01]: He's 88.

88
00:07:24,354 --> 00:07:28,397
[SPEAKER_01]: But this is the second Mrs. Faber, and she's probably in her late 50s or early 60s.

89
00:07:28,437 --> 00:07:33,140
[SPEAKER_01]: She said she played golf 50 few years, but she didn't look that old.

90
00:07:33,620 --> 00:07:35,481
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe she started when she was one year old.

91
00:07:35,521 --> 00:07:36,222
[SPEAKER_01]: It'd be all right.

92
00:07:37,747 --> 00:07:41,109
[SPEAKER_01]: She didn't look like she'd be 60 or 65.

93
00:07:42,310 --> 00:07:43,971
[SPEAKER_01]: She was very well-groomed.

94
00:07:44,011 --> 00:07:54,117
[SPEAKER_01]: She goes to the hairdresser regularly, I understand, even because she hates to leave the house now because red favors, so needs attention constantly.

95
00:07:54,317 --> 00:07:58,520
[SPEAKER_01]: He's not helpless or feeble, but you just can't take a chance, she says.

96
00:07:58,600 --> 00:08:06,285
[SPEAKER_01]: And so when she went to the hairdresser's, that's when he went out to get the mail, and there's some pieces on the

97
00:08:07,447 --> 00:08:17,291
[SPEAKER_01]: The miss slipped out of the mailbox, fell on the slab there, and he tried to pick him up, fell over and hurt his ankle and his wrist, which still had a big bandage on it.

98
00:08:17,371 --> 00:08:19,051
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why he couldn't write very well on the ball.

99
00:08:19,091 --> 00:08:24,013
[SPEAKER_01]: I got an autographed ball, and the first autograph he wrote looks like hieroglyphics.

100
00:08:24,694 --> 00:08:27,215
[SPEAKER_01]: He wrote it again, and it looks a little bit better, recognizable.

101
00:08:29,395 --> 00:08:35,438
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's a very nice house, well maintained, at least the inside.

102
00:08:35,458 --> 00:08:36,198
[SPEAKER_01]: The yard was

103
00:08:36,894 --> 00:08:39,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Fairly maintained, a few things here and there.

104
00:08:40,416 --> 00:08:41,476
[SPEAKER_01]: But it was nice.

105
00:08:42,436 --> 00:08:45,978
[SPEAKER_01]: And nice furniture, carpeting.

106
00:08:47,078 --> 00:08:52,260
[SPEAKER_01]: And Red Faber came out in the living room to greet me in his pajamas and robe, and she introduced me to him.

107
00:08:53,041 --> 00:08:55,562
[SPEAKER_01]: And she said, let's go into the back room where he likes to sit.

108
00:08:55,722 --> 00:09:02,485
[SPEAKER_01]: So we went into the back room and passed by a dining room and a bedroom and a bathroom.

109
00:09:02,505 --> 00:09:03,545
[SPEAKER_01]: We got in the back room.

110
00:09:06,382 --> 00:09:17,971
[SPEAKER_01]: He sat in the chair there and I sat on a couch across from him, put the tape recorder on a little table in the middle, and I started firing away, probably around quarter to eleven.

111
00:09:19,152 --> 00:09:21,214
[SPEAKER_01]: I got it set up.

112
00:09:21,274 --> 00:09:23,856
[SPEAKER_01]: I could see right away this was going to be a very difficult interview.

113
00:09:23,916 --> 00:09:25,857
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't remember much and didn't talk much.

114
00:09:27,298 --> 00:09:30,581
[SPEAKER_01]: I shot my bullet rather quickly.

115
00:09:30,861 --> 00:09:32,042
[SPEAKER_01]: His memory was bad.

116
00:09:32,162 --> 00:09:35,885
[SPEAKER_01]: I asked him about the Chicago Black Sox and all the

117
00:09:36,791 --> 00:09:41,273
[SPEAKER_01]: the feeling that existed on the team then, you know, as these guys were throwing games and so on.

118
00:09:41,854 --> 00:09:45,075
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, oh, that was a pretty good team, good spirit on the team.

119
00:09:45,095 --> 00:09:52,039
[SPEAKER_01]: So I realized right away it wasn't much use for real meaty kinds of things I wanted.

120
00:09:52,519 --> 00:09:54,620
[SPEAKER_01]: I couldn't press him on these things.

121
00:09:54,660 --> 00:09:57,021
[SPEAKER_01]: So I played it very loosely.

122
00:09:57,101 --> 00:09:58,962
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything he could say then was welcome.

123
00:09:59,122 --> 00:10:02,224
[SPEAKER_01]: I skipped over his whole record and he didn't say much.

124
00:10:02,884 --> 00:10:04,205
[SPEAKER_01]: I did most of the talking, I'm sure.

125
00:10:05,600 --> 00:10:08,341
[SPEAKER_01]: If I want an interview of myself, I've probably got one on this tape.

126
00:10:10,602 --> 00:10:12,243
[SPEAKER_01]: She wouldn't come over and sit with us.

127
00:10:12,263 --> 00:10:18,465
[SPEAKER_01]: She sat in a chair about 10 or 12 feet behind the couch and listened in very closely and tossed in her two cents.

128
00:10:18,625 --> 00:10:19,685
[SPEAKER_01]: I wish she'd been right with us.

129
00:10:19,745 --> 00:10:21,466
[SPEAKER_01]: I asked her to, but she didn't want to.

130
00:10:21,906 --> 00:10:23,947
[SPEAKER_01]: Probably the tape recorder didn't pick it up very well.

131
00:10:24,027 --> 00:10:31,350
[SPEAKER_01]: I did, at the end, get her to sit in with us on the first part of this tape and fill in a few of the gaps, which she finally did.

132
00:10:35,654 --> 00:10:38,276
[SPEAKER_01]: He's 88 years old.

133
00:10:38,376 --> 00:10:40,217
[SPEAKER_01]: He's a nice guy.

134
00:10:40,257 --> 00:10:43,039
[SPEAKER_01]: He's pleasant, friendly, as nice as could be.

135
00:10:43,119 --> 00:10:43,940
[SPEAKER_01]: I just don't see...

136
00:10:44,180 --> 00:10:46,281
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't seem like a tough competitor to me.

137
00:10:46,321 --> 00:10:47,262
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe he was, but...

138
00:10:48,123 --> 00:10:48,843
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm sure he was.

139
00:10:49,224 --> 00:10:50,464
[SPEAKER_01]: But he was a very nice guy.

140
00:10:50,484 --> 00:10:54,067
[SPEAKER_01]: I said he never got thrown out of a game and never had trouble with umpires or much.

141
00:10:54,687 --> 00:10:57,529
[SPEAKER_01]: Even that perfect game he lost.

142
00:10:57,569 --> 00:11:01,192
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he seemed like a real fine person.

143
00:11:02,193 --> 00:11:02,593
[SPEAKER_01]: And...

144
00:11:04,196 --> 00:11:10,979
[SPEAKER_01]: I just wish I'd gotten to him earlier when we could have gotten into some of these things more fully.

145
00:11:11,700 --> 00:11:14,721
[SPEAKER_01]: So it was a very disappointing interview, but at least I did talk with him.

146
00:11:15,381 --> 00:11:20,184
[SPEAKER_01]: As far as pictures go, I don't know if the tape recorder might have been on while I was taking some pictures or talking about the pictures.

147
00:11:20,844 --> 00:11:26,747
[SPEAKER_01]: Mrs. Faber, apparently his first wife, died in the early 40s.

148
00:11:28,528 --> 00:11:39,958
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he met, second is his favorite, I think she tells that, I forgot what it was, through some friends, in 43 or so, and they got married in 47, I guess.

149
00:11:40,018 --> 00:11:43,882
[SPEAKER_01]: She is, I don't know, just what her antecedents were.

150
00:11:46,804 --> 00:11:47,805
[SPEAKER_01]: She grew up in Chicago.

151
00:11:49,046 --> 00:11:50,788
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess that would probably be her only marriage.

152
00:11:54,189 --> 00:11:56,331
[SPEAKER_01]: She's got a full-time job now taking care of him.

153
00:11:56,351 --> 00:11:56,972
[SPEAKER_01]: She doesn't work.

154
00:11:56,992 --> 00:11:58,593
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know if she did work one time.

155
00:11:58,633 --> 00:12:05,760
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe she did.

156
00:12:06,561 --> 00:12:12,466
[SPEAKER_01]: She helped out filling in some gaps and things here and there, which helped somewhat.

157
00:12:12,626 --> 00:12:14,908
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, she knew nothing about his baseball career.

158
00:12:14,948 --> 00:12:17,291
[SPEAKER_01]: He'd been out of baseball ten years before she ever met him.

159
00:12:18,862 --> 00:12:20,162
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh yeah, I was talking about the scrapbooks.

160
00:12:20,722 --> 00:12:26,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Apparently, she said somebody got a hold of one of her scrapbooks and took off with him, whether he gave them away.

161
00:12:26,184 --> 00:12:29,124
[SPEAKER_01]: He's the kind of a guy who wouldn't care much and let things get out.

162
00:12:29,824 --> 00:12:31,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Someone like Fred Blake would, I imagine.

163
00:12:32,785 --> 00:12:35,765
[SPEAKER_01]: And maybe Woody Inglis, or not Inglis, maybe Carmen Hill.

164
00:12:35,785 --> 00:12:40,246
[SPEAKER_01]: But when she got them in 1943 or 1944, there wasn't too much left.

165
00:12:40,286 --> 00:12:45,547
[SPEAKER_01]: And she salvaged what there was left and has put them in scrapbooks and has kept things going pretty well since then.

166
00:12:46,328 --> 00:12:48,108
[SPEAKER_01]: So what he has now is largely due to her.

167
00:12:48,525 --> 00:12:57,611
[SPEAKER_01]: She brought out this old scrapbook and a lot of pictures which he said he had never seen for years.

168
00:12:57,631 --> 00:12:58,351
[SPEAKER_01]: She was out of the room.

169
00:12:58,451 --> 00:12:59,972
[SPEAKER_01]: I was looking through the scrapbook.

170
00:13:00,013 --> 00:13:09,939
[SPEAKER_01]: I found a picture, a kind of a sepia formal portrait or photograph of Favreau after he was through baseball, a very nice one.

171
00:13:10,119 --> 00:13:10,820
[SPEAKER_01]: There were two copies.

172
00:13:10,920 --> 00:13:12,581
[SPEAKER_01]: One was mounted in the book and there was a loose one.

173
00:13:13,650 --> 00:13:20,255
[SPEAKER_01]: I asked him, she was gone, I asked him, would you mind, if you have two of these, would either of you have one of these?

174
00:13:20,315 --> 00:13:21,215
[SPEAKER_01]: Could you autograph it?

175
00:13:21,255 --> 00:13:23,537
[SPEAKER_01]: And I handed it to him, he was agreeable.

176
00:13:23,877 --> 00:13:26,839
[SPEAKER_01]: And she walked back in the room about that time and she kind of blew up.

177
00:13:26,939 --> 00:13:28,120
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh no, you can't do that.

178
00:13:28,620 --> 00:13:30,642
[SPEAKER_01]: The son may want this copy and so on.

179
00:13:30,662 --> 00:13:32,403
[SPEAKER_01]: So I was kind of embarrassed about this.

180
00:13:32,463 --> 00:13:36,206
[SPEAKER_01]: I had no intention of trying to do something behind her back.

181
00:13:37,844 --> 00:13:41,347
[SPEAKER_01]: She apologized profusely, and I think she forgot about it.

182
00:13:41,627 --> 00:13:42,868
[SPEAKER_01]: We got along very well after that.

183
00:13:43,308 --> 00:14:00,181
[SPEAKER_01]: But I did get out my camera, the 35mm camera, and we cleared off the table between us a little bit, got some outdoor light and some of the indoor light, and I took about 14 or 15 pictures of himself and a few of the old players that he had in the scrapbook.

184
00:14:00,242 --> 00:14:02,143
[SPEAKER_01]: Nice big 8x10s, they'd be great to have.

185
00:14:02,183 --> 00:14:03,344
[SPEAKER_01]: I hope they do come out.

186
00:14:03,364 --> 00:14:05,866
[SPEAKER_01]: I tried to remember everything to do with that 35mm camera.

187
00:14:08,796 --> 00:14:10,457
[SPEAKER_01]: So we got some pictures there.

188
00:14:10,697 --> 00:14:12,757
[SPEAKER_01]: There wasn't too much else in the scrapbook.

189
00:14:12,777 --> 00:14:20,160
[SPEAKER_01]: A lot of it was post-career and old-timers conventions and things like that.

190
00:14:20,180 --> 00:14:23,261
[SPEAKER_01]: So actually, the scrapbook was a limit.

191
00:14:23,361 --> 00:14:30,404
[SPEAKER_01]: As much as I like scrapbooks, even if I'd gotten that one and somehow I got it, it wouldn't have been that valuable to me.

192
00:14:31,124 --> 00:14:34,686
[SPEAKER_01]: But the pictures, I thought, were pretty good, assuming they do come out.

193
00:14:34,726 --> 00:14:36,566
[SPEAKER_01]: So I don't have any autographed picture of him at all.

194
00:14:36,626 --> 00:14:38,227
[SPEAKER_01]: I got him to autograph the ball.

195
00:14:39,698 --> 00:14:52,722
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't want to autograph anything after I saw him writing because he couldn't write, and what would be the good of having a totally distorted autograph like that?

196
00:15:00,116 --> 00:15:02,578
[SPEAKER_01]: That takes care of the kind of interview I thought it was.

197
00:15:02,878 --> 00:15:04,099
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm glad I went out there.

198
00:15:04,119 --> 00:15:10,583
[SPEAKER_01]: I certainly had to see him, but as far as getting any inside information, there was just almost nothing.

199
00:15:10,603 --> 00:15:18,668
[SPEAKER_01]: The most critical points of his career, he had false memories or no memories.

200
00:15:18,708 --> 00:15:21,270
[SPEAKER_01]: Just nothing to...

201
00:15:22,743 --> 00:15:25,205
[SPEAKER_01]: I just couldn't seem to sink into it.

202
00:15:25,225 --> 00:15:29,648
[SPEAKER_01]: It reminds me of what Debbie Glaston must have gone through in her oral history interview with Alan Weinstein.

203
00:15:29,688 --> 00:15:31,670
[SPEAKER_01]: I really don't remember anything.

204
00:15:31,770 --> 00:15:33,992
[SPEAKER_01]: A total frustration to feel.

205
00:15:34,012 --> 00:15:39,076
[SPEAKER_01]: You've got so much to ask, and they've got so little to tell you.

206
00:15:40,917 --> 00:15:47,542
[SPEAKER_01]: There were two things I promised to look up for them.

207
00:15:50,575 --> 00:15:56,276
[SPEAKER_01]: What game was it that he pitched only 67 pitches, which is the record number of minimum pitches for a game?

208
00:15:58,037 --> 00:16:01,758
[SPEAKER_01]: And I promised to get that data and send it back to them.

209
00:16:02,818 --> 00:16:06,039
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe it would be, he said, maybe against Washington.

210
00:16:06,059 --> 00:16:11,460
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, also, his near-perfect game, where he had two out in the ninth inning, and nobody had reached base.

211
00:16:12,820 --> 00:16:14,380
[SPEAKER_01]: Jack Lapp was the hitter.

212
00:16:14,560 --> 00:16:17,581
[SPEAKER_01]: So this was against the Philadelphia Athletics, and Lapp hit a boundary.

213
00:16:17,661 --> 00:16:22,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Eddie Collins hit through to first, and Lapp was safe, called safe for the umpire.

214
00:16:23,723 --> 00:16:25,003
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he got the next guy out.

215
00:16:25,023 --> 00:16:27,264
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's the 28th batter, allowed one hit.

216
00:16:27,844 --> 00:16:30,805
[SPEAKER_01]: He thinks these two games might have been the same game.

217
00:16:32,865 --> 00:16:39,127
[SPEAKER_01]: So I've got to check up on that and find out if this was the game, when it was, who it was against.

218
00:16:39,207 --> 00:16:41,868
[SPEAKER_01]: If there were two games, get the dates on those.

219
00:16:43,425 --> 00:16:49,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Xerox box scores of those games and send them back to them.

220
00:16:50,471 --> 00:16:56,236
[SPEAKER_01]: Mrs. Faber, the president of Mrs. Faber, is quite interested in baseball.

221
00:16:56,256 --> 00:16:57,717
[SPEAKER_01]: There are a number of baseball books.

222
00:16:57,757 --> 00:17:01,400
[SPEAKER_01]: There's the Macmillan second edition encyclopedia.

223
00:17:01,440 --> 00:17:05,303
[SPEAKER_01]: There's one of the recent Turkin-Thompson encyclopedias.

