448
00:30:08,780 --> 00:30:09,200
[SPEAKER_02]: Uh-huh.

449
00:30:10,100 --> 00:30:14,062
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you just happen to develop it, or how did it... Oh, no, I practiced it.

450
00:30:14,362 --> 00:30:17,503
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you learn from any of the old spitters?

451
00:30:18,443 --> 00:30:19,503
[SPEAKER_02]: Old spitball pitchers?

452
00:30:19,624 --> 00:30:21,104
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I've learned from many of them.

453
00:30:21,324 --> 00:30:23,805
[SPEAKER_00]: They quit letting them pitch.

454
00:30:24,703 --> 00:30:28,124
[SPEAKER_00]: excepting those that were using it at the time.

455
00:30:28,544 --> 00:30:30,544
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think I was about the last one.

456
00:30:30,564 --> 00:30:31,244
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I think so.

457
00:30:31,584 --> 00:30:33,084
[SPEAKER_00]: I got a story here about that.

458
00:30:36,525 --> 00:30:42,166
[SPEAKER_02]: Burleigh Grimes and Kovaleski were a couple of those that were pitching at the time.

459
00:30:42,186 --> 00:30:45,407
[SPEAKER_02]: Kovaleski was a good ball pitcher.

460
00:30:46,287 --> 00:30:47,807
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you throw every pitch a spitter?

461
00:30:48,427 --> 00:30:49,007
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, no.

462
00:30:50,867 --> 00:30:52,068
[SPEAKER_00]: How often did you throw it?

463
00:30:52,648 --> 00:30:53,888
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I'd throw it pretty often.

464
00:30:54,985 --> 00:30:58,566
[SPEAKER_00]: But I could get the ball over pretty good, you know.

465
00:31:00,646 --> 00:31:02,807
[SPEAKER_00]: And they hit them in the dirt more than anything.

466
00:31:03,447 --> 00:31:05,508
