537
00:34:26,962 --> 00:34:28,722
[SPEAKER_02]: No, I don't think so.

538
00:34:30,943 --> 00:34:32,384
[SPEAKER_00]: You were very agreeable, weren't you?

539
00:34:33,244 --> 00:34:37,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, well, money at that time, you know, a guy's $10,000 is a hell of a salary.

540
00:34:40,186 --> 00:34:43,107
[SPEAKER_02]: I guess Eddie Collins probably got as much as anybody did, didn't he?

541
00:34:43,147 --> 00:34:45,368
[SPEAKER_02]: He got maybe $14,000 or $15,000.

542
00:34:50,030 --> 00:34:51,310
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you ever meet Ben Johnson?

543
00:34:52,922 --> 00:34:53,963
[SPEAKER_02]: Name of the league president?

544
00:34:54,303 --> 00:34:56,684
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I don't know.

545
00:35:01,267 --> 00:35:01,988
[SPEAKER_00]: I couldn't say.

546
00:35:02,849 --> 00:35:04,870
[SPEAKER_00]: But I saw some of his meetings.

547
00:35:04,990 --> 00:35:07,952
[SPEAKER_00]: I was there, you know.

548
00:35:11,414 --> 00:35:13,095
[SPEAKER_02]: No, I don't know.

549
00:35:13,295 --> 00:35:15,277
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm writing a book about Ben Jensen.

550
00:35:15,357 --> 00:35:18,259
[SPEAKER_02]: You see, he went to Marietta College briefly back in the 80s.

551
00:35:19,638 --> 00:35:23,942
[SPEAKER_02]: He left some money to the school, and they built a field house, which is named after him.

552
00:35:24,842 --> 00:35:30,607
[SPEAKER_02]: And nobody knows who he was anymore, so I'm trying to restore his memory to the modern generation.

553
00:35:31,368 --> 00:35:38,934
[SPEAKER_02]: And so I'm writing this book, and I'm trying to find people who may have known him, could tell me a little bit about him, some of the personal side, you know.

554
00:35:38,975 --> 00:35:42,798
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't get too well-acquainted, as I'm afraid, you know.

555
00:35:44,914 --> 00:35:47,375
[SPEAKER_02]: Unless you didn't behave very well and you went into his office.

556
00:35:47,395 --> 00:35:54,257
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't think I ever did that.

557
00:35:55,537 --> 00:36:04,720
[SPEAKER_02]: During 1918, the war year, so many of the players went into the shipyards and the steel mills.

558
00:36:04,740 --> 00:36:05,340
[SPEAKER_02]: Remember that?

559
00:36:05,761 --> 00:36:07,461
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, there were a few.

560
00:36:08,161 --> 00:36:10,382
[SPEAKER_00]: The Sox kept a pretty good ball club.

561
00:36:12,807 --> 00:36:13,867
[SPEAKER_00]: I had to go up there.

562
00:36:14,207 --> 00:36:17,808
[SPEAKER_00]: I pitched, I think, about four or five games or something like that.

563
00:36:18,528 --> 00:36:21,249
[SPEAKER_00]: And I went right on up there.

564
00:36:21,749 --> 00:36:23,730
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you pitch while you were in the Navy?

565
00:36:24,170 --> 00:36:25,190
[SPEAKER_02]: You got a weekend off?

566
00:36:25,210 --> 00:36:26,370
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, I would pitch.

567
00:36:26,850 --> 00:36:27,610
[SPEAKER_00]: Not too much.

568
00:36:27,630 --> 00:36:28,871
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, with the White Sox.

569
00:36:29,751 --> 00:36:32,431
[SPEAKER_02]: Could you pitch while you were in the Navy and get a weekend pass?

570
00:36:32,592 --> 00:36:33,332
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, no, no.

571
00:36:33,372 --> 00:36:33,872
[SPEAKER_02]: I see.

572
00:36:34,652 --> 00:36:39,053
[SPEAKER_02]: I think Bob Shockey said that he did that for a while.

573
00:36:39,842 --> 00:36:43,485
[SPEAKER_02]: He was in the Navy over in Philadelphia, I guess, and he could get a weekend pass.

574
00:36:43,505 --> 00:36:45,506
[SPEAKER_02]: He'd go and pitch for the Yankees.

575
00:36:45,586 --> 00:36:46,928
[SPEAKER_02]: I never did.

576
00:36:47,788 --> 00:36:51,931
[SPEAKER_00]: And I wasn't too far away, either, you know.

577
00:36:52,092 --> 00:36:53,693
[SPEAKER_02]: He could have used you, I'm sure, that year.

578
00:36:55,414 --> 00:37:05,742
