795
00:49:12,533 --> 00:49:13,934
[SPEAKER_02]: Right by your right elbow, huh?

796
00:49:14,414 --> 00:49:14,874
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

797
00:49:15,815 --> 00:49:17,536
[SPEAKER_02]: And took it out?

798
00:49:17,696 --> 00:49:18,016
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

799
00:49:19,077 --> 00:49:27,242
[SPEAKER_02]: I would think that would very—might very well have hurt your throat there, but it's been a good operation.

800
00:49:27,262 --> 00:49:30,484
[SPEAKER_02]: But it wasn't too long, you know, and I fished that same year.

801
00:49:30,544 --> 00:49:33,566
[SPEAKER_02]: Mm-hmm.

802
00:49:33,606 --> 00:49:35,027
[SPEAKER_02]: Did some specialist did that?

803
00:49:36,363 --> 00:49:49,734
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, they had good doctors in those days, you know.

804
00:49:50,354 --> 00:49:57,780
[SPEAKER_00]: They, I don't know, they didn't hesitate about doing anything, you know.

805
00:49:57,800 --> 00:50:03,144
[SPEAKER_00]: They saw that was necessary, so they went ahead and took it out.

806
00:50:06,093 --> 00:50:07,674
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you ever hear of Bonesetter Reese?

807
00:50:08,054 --> 00:50:08,574
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

808
00:50:10,615 --> 00:50:12,096
[SPEAKER_02]: He fixed a lot of arms, I guess.

809
00:50:12,116 --> 00:50:13,377
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, he did.

810
00:50:17,619 --> 00:50:20,300
[SPEAKER_02]: I think I still remember how it went to him once.

811
00:50:21,881 --> 00:50:23,261
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

812
00:50:23,281 --> 00:50:24,242
[SPEAKER_02]: He's over in Youngstown.

813
00:50:25,583 --> 00:50:28,704
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I think.

814
00:50:28,764 --> 00:50:32,706
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know how long ago.

815
00:50:32,786 --> 00:50:34,607
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm pretty sure I went to him one time.

816
00:50:35,546 --> 00:50:35,586
[SPEAKER_03]: Q.

817
00:50:35,606 --> 00:50:38,449
[SPEAKER_03]: You had your knee operated on once for cartilage.

818
00:50:38,469 --> 00:50:39,530
[SPEAKER_03]: Was he the one then?

819
00:50:41,652 --> 00:50:43,373
[SPEAKER_00]: A. Oh, I think it was Mom.

820
00:50:43,393 --> 00:50:43,533
[SPEAKER_00]: Q.

821
00:50:43,613 --> 00:50:49,418
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, you had your knee, too, that had cartilage out of it.

822
00:50:49,439 --> 00:50:52,061
[SPEAKER_00]: A. Yeah, that's scars, though.

823
00:50:53,242 --> 00:50:54,463
[SPEAKER_00]: That was a bad one.

824
00:50:55,444 --> 00:50:56,905
[SPEAKER_00]: Lots of cartilage in there.

825
00:50:56,925 --> 00:50:58,106
[SPEAKER_00]: I had to take that out.

826
00:51:00,980 --> 00:51:05,701
[SPEAKER_00]: That was quite a while pitching before I was able to go in there.

827
00:51:06,821 --> 00:51:08,501
[SPEAKER_00]: I forget now which year that was.

828
00:51:15,342 --> 00:51:22,484
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you had pretty good control, I guess, huh?

829
00:51:22,884 --> 00:51:24,764
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, it wasn't too bad.

830
00:51:25,384 --> 00:51:30,665
[SPEAKER_02]: You always struck out more than you walked, and you never walked too many here, but more strikeouts.

831
00:51:32,865 --> 00:51:39,409
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

832
00:51:40,450 --> 00:51:42,231
[SPEAKER_02]: How about some of these hitters you faced?

833
00:51:42,711 --> 00:51:45,172
[SPEAKER_02]: Who were some of the tough ones there?

834
00:51:45,473 --> 00:51:49,495
[SPEAKER_02]: Well... How did you pitch Ty Cobb?

835
00:51:50,916 --> 00:51:53,778
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I didn't have too much trouble with him.

836
00:51:55,298 --> 00:51:58,640
[SPEAKER_00]: I think I was about as good as anybody that pitched to him.

837
00:51:59,641 --> 00:52:01,262
[SPEAKER_00]: I struck him out quite a few times.

838
00:52:03,467 --> 00:52:03,587
[SPEAKER_00]: Q.

839
00:52:04,168 --> 00:52:05,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Did he hit your spitter much?

840
00:52:05,348 --> 00:52:06,949
[SPEAKER_00]: A. Oh, yeah.

841
00:52:06,989 --> 00:52:08,410
[SPEAKER_00]: He threw him a lot of spitters.

842
00:52:09,411 --> 00:52:14,874
[SPEAKER_00]: I threw a spitter, some sidearm, overhand.

843
00:52:15,435 --> 00:52:18,897
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, I'd throw it any way I was thrown.

844
00:52:21,379 --> 00:52:31,385
[SPEAKER_00]: And like a sidearm spitter, I'd throw it to a left-hander, and then hit his hand and put it in his mouth.

845
00:52:34,037 --> 00:52:39,621
[SPEAKER_00]: up there, all of a sudden, it's sharp basically.

846
00:52:40,102 --> 00:52:43,504
[SPEAKER_00]: I had a fellow swinging that ball, and by gosh, I bet you missed him that far.

847
00:52:43,524 --> 00:52:46,026
[SPEAKER_00]: About two feet.

848
00:52:47,627 --> 00:52:49,729
[SPEAKER_02]: How would you pitch Cobb, any particular way?

849
00:52:50,069 --> 00:52:54,112
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I used to mix him up.

850
00:52:54,533 --> 00:52:56,774
[SPEAKER_00]: How about Ruth, the babe?

851
00:52:58,781 --> 00:52:59,722
[SPEAKER_00]: He was tough, too.

852
00:52:59,802 --> 00:53:01,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Not too tough, though, for me.

853
00:53:01,423 --> 00:53:02,143
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know.

854
00:53:02,744 --> 00:53:04,785
[SPEAKER_00]: I seem to have pretty good stuff on me.

855
00:53:05,146 --> 00:53:06,547
[SPEAKER_00]: Keep it low and away, huh?

856
00:53:06,827 --> 00:53:12,311
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I'd throw it over the place, but I was breaking down on him, you know.

857
00:53:12,891 --> 00:53:14,152
[SPEAKER_00]: He hit a lot on the ground.

858
00:53:23,999 --> 00:53:27,902
[SPEAKER_02]: How about some of the, well,

859
00:53:28,377 --> 00:53:30,898
[SPEAKER_02]: Who were some of the good right-handed hitters you pitched against?

860
00:53:31,178 --> 00:53:32,898
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, gee, I don't even remember.

861
00:53:34,399 --> 00:53:35,439
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll tell you.

862
00:53:35,479 --> 00:53:37,680
[SPEAKER_02]: Harry Heilman, was he right or he hit left?

863
00:53:37,700 --> 00:53:40,240
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, he hit right-handed.

864
00:53:40,261 --> 00:53:41,161
[SPEAKER_00]: He was a pretty good hitter.

865
00:53:41,301 --> 00:53:48,223
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm thinking of George Sistler, but I guess he's a left-handed hitter.

866
00:53:49,423 --> 00:53:49,763
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

867
00:53:54,285 --> 00:53:55,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, he was a great hitter.

868
00:54:00,210 --> 00:54:04,352
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, how'd you get along with the umpires?

869
00:54:05,373 --> 00:54:06,794
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I never had too much trouble.

870
00:54:06,954 --> 00:54:08,214
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you ever get thrown out of a game?

871
00:54:09,595 --> 00:54:11,176
[SPEAKER_02]: No, I don't believe I did.

872
00:54:11,216 --> 00:54:12,257
[SPEAKER_02]: That's quite an achievement.

873
00:54:13,897 --> 00:54:18,840
[SPEAKER_02]: Not many players can run as long as you were.

874
00:54:22,502 --> 00:54:26,084
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you have any favorite umpires or did you like them all?

875
00:54:26,844 --> 00:54:27,785
[SPEAKER_02]: How about Billy Evans?

876
00:54:29,095 --> 00:54:30,577
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, he was a pretty good umpire.

877
00:54:31,177 --> 00:54:35,842
[SPEAKER_00]: And I never thought I was going to get what his name was.

878
00:54:36,643 --> 00:54:38,104
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought he was a good umpire.

879
00:54:41,288 --> 00:54:43,730
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, shot, that name's all disappeared on me.

880
00:54:43,750 --> 00:54:44,251
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

881
00:54:46,433 --> 00:54:48,355
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you think the umpires really, uh...

882
00:54:50,088 --> 00:54:52,469
[SPEAKER_02]: do a pretty good, fair job overall?

883
00:54:52,489 --> 00:54:53,130
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, they do.

884
00:54:54,070 --> 00:55:01,094
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a tough job, but... Yeah, some of them were no good at all, but most of them...

885
00:55:02,034 --> 00:55:04,155
[SPEAKER_00]: They got rid of them, I guess, huh?

886
00:55:04,296 --> 00:55:05,376
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

887
00:55:05,496 --> 00:55:13,981
[SPEAKER_00]: After you pitched to them, I got us in order to tell which way they were going to call them.

888
00:55:14,001 --> 00:55:17,503
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you ever remember an Ollie Chill?

889
00:55:18,761 --> 00:55:19,501
[SPEAKER_02]: Ali Chill?

890
00:55:19,901 --> 00:55:20,401
[SPEAKER_02]: Chill?

891
00:55:20,441 --> 00:55:21,201
[SPEAKER_02]: He's an umpire.

892
00:55:21,241 --> 00:55:21,502
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

893
00:55:21,522 --> 00:55:23,222
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you remember him very well?

894
00:55:23,242 --> 00:55:23,342
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

895
00:55:23,822 --> 00:55:24,742
[SPEAKER_02]: I guess he wasn't too good.

896
00:55:25,122 --> 00:55:26,722
[SPEAKER_02]: He wasn't too bad.

897
00:55:30,263 --> 00:55:37,704
[SPEAKER_02]: He got fired by Ben Johnson, so I figured he might have been not the greatest.

898
00:55:39,945 --> 00:55:45,126
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I guess he had charge of all of them.

899
00:55:45,206 --> 00:55:46,266
[SPEAKER_02]: How about Tommy Connolly?

900
00:55:46,286 --> 00:55:47,066
[SPEAKER_02]: Remember him at all?

901
00:55:47,943 --> 00:55:48,764
[SPEAKER_01]: Tommy Connelly?

902
00:55:50,105 --> 00:55:50,505
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah.

903
00:55:50,545 --> 00:55:51,486
[SPEAKER_02]: He's an old umpire.

904
00:55:51,506 --> 00:55:55,710
[SPEAKER_02]: He's a pretty good one, I guess.

905
00:55:56,871 --> 00:55:59,473
[SPEAKER_02]: Where'd you live in Chicago when you were with the White Sox?

906
00:55:59,493 --> 00:56:00,413
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you have a home here?

907
00:56:01,014 --> 00:56:05,237
[SPEAKER_02]: Or did you just rent a place?

908
00:56:05,478 --> 00:56:09,341
[SPEAKER_00]: I was in rooms, you know.

909
00:56:09,421 --> 00:56:13,224
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess I wasn't married at the time.

910
00:56:13,264 --> 00:56:17,047
[SPEAKER_03]: The Southern Hotel.

911
00:56:17,642 --> 00:56:18,483
[SPEAKER_00]: Sutherland Hotel?

912
00:56:19,283 --> 00:56:20,304
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

913
00:56:21,005 --> 00:56:23,567
[SPEAKER_02]: That's where the ballplayers stayed, was it?

914
00:56:23,967 --> 00:56:25,568
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, quite a few of them.

915
00:56:26,129 --> 00:56:27,650
[SPEAKER_02]: Not very far from the ballpark?

916
00:56:27,850 --> 00:56:28,650
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

917
00:56:28,971 --> 00:56:34,435
[SPEAKER_03]: Forty-third and Drexel, maybe?

918
00:56:34,455 --> 00:56:36,777
[SPEAKER_03]: I think it was 47.

919
00:56:45,414 --> 00:56:48,756
[SPEAKER_02]: In the ballparks at 35th, is that right?

920
00:56:48,816 --> 00:56:50,677
[SPEAKER_02]: No, 35th.

921
00:56:53,678 --> 00:57:01,122
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, did you, what did you do in the off-season?

922
00:57:02,343 --> 00:57:02,943
[SPEAKER_02]: A lot of hunting?

923
00:57:03,403 --> 00:57:03,643
[SPEAKER_02]: Fishing?

924
00:57:03,723 --> 00:57:04,964
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I did a lot of hunting, yeah.

925
00:57:04,984 --> 00:57:05,844
[SPEAKER_00]: You're a hunter, huh?

926
00:57:05,865 --> 00:57:08,466
[SPEAKER_00]: So good, my guns up there.

927
00:57:08,866 --> 00:57:10,067
[SPEAKER_00]: They're inside there.

928
00:57:11,983 --> 00:57:14,904
[SPEAKER_00]: I think the lab has got one of the guns.

929
00:57:18,686 --> 00:57:20,447
[SPEAKER_00]: Where'd you go hunting?

930
00:57:20,467 --> 00:57:22,108
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, out in the woods, around the place.

931
00:57:22,788 --> 00:57:25,529
[SPEAKER_02]: You mean around here, or out in Iowa, or back home?

932
00:57:25,689 --> 00:57:28,650
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, out in Iowa, I used to go a lot hunting.

933
00:57:28,790 --> 00:57:33,913
[SPEAKER_00]: But then when I moved into Chicago, I went to a lot of places to hunt, too.

934
00:57:34,053 --> 00:57:36,814
[SPEAKER_00]: Any of your fellow players you go hunting with?

935
00:57:36,974 --> 00:57:41,156
[SPEAKER_00]: Who are some of your... Well, no, they weren't around in the wintertime, you know.

936
00:57:43,100 --> 00:57:44,161
[SPEAKER_00]: Ray Shaw hunt much?

937
00:57:46,682 --> 00:57:47,883
[SPEAKER_02]: He ran a bowling alley, huh?

938
00:57:49,504 --> 00:57:50,545
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what he did.

939
00:57:53,647 --> 00:58:00,571
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you ever get a job in the off-season or did you just hunt and take it easy?

940
00:58:01,271 --> 00:58:02,932
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I don't believe I ever got a job.

941
00:58:04,573 --> 00:58:06,394
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, once in a while I guess I did.

942
00:58:10,377 --> 00:58:12,218
[SPEAKER_02]: How about after you retired, what'd you do?

943
00:58:13,773 --> 00:58:17,014
[SPEAKER_02]: I got a story here about when you retired.

944
00:58:19,275 --> 00:58:22,156
[SPEAKER_02]: I guess 33 was your last season, wasn't it?

945
00:58:22,196 --> 00:58:22,556
[SPEAKER_02]: 1933.

946
00:58:23,216 --> 00:58:23,596
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

947
00:58:24,057 --> 00:58:25,617
[SPEAKER_02]: I wondered what you did then.

948
00:58:26,878 --> 00:58:27,678
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know.

949
00:58:27,718 --> 00:58:29,919
[SPEAKER_03]: He had bowling alley and baseball.

950
00:58:30,019 --> 00:58:30,679
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, yeah.

951
00:58:30,999 --> 00:58:32,299
[SPEAKER_02]: That's right.

952
00:58:32,319 --> 00:58:33,320
[SPEAKER_02]: He had a bowling alley.

953
00:58:35,360 --> 00:58:36,361
[SPEAKER_02]: How long did you have that?

954
00:58:36,921 --> 00:58:37,881
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I forget now.

955
00:58:38,381 --> 00:58:39,222
[SPEAKER_02]: A few years.

956
00:58:39,522 --> 00:58:42,743
[SPEAKER_03]: He was 46, and then he went to coaching.

957
00:58:43,415 --> 00:58:44,256
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I see.

958
00:58:45,837 --> 00:58:46,477
[SPEAKER_02]: Where'd you coach?

959
00:58:48,539 --> 00:58:50,640
[SPEAKER_03]: For the White Sox for three years.

960
00:58:50,780 --> 00:58:51,621
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, I see.

961
00:58:51,641 --> 00:58:52,221
[SPEAKER_03]: 46, 7, and 8.

962
00:58:53,462 --> 00:58:54,122
[SPEAKER_00]: I see.

963
00:58:54,142 --> 00:58:56,084
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't forget them years.

964
00:59:00,146 --> 00:59:01,908
[SPEAKER_03]: When Teddy Lyons was manager.

965
00:59:02,488 --> 00:59:03,008
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

966
00:59:03,609 --> 00:59:07,771
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, Teddy's a good...

967
00:59:08,632 --> 00:59:09,573
[SPEAKER_00]: He's a good baby.

968
00:59:09,873 --> 00:59:10,013
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

969
00:59:10,073 --> 00:59:10,854
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't think so.

970
00:59:11,014 --> 00:59:11,294
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

971
00:59:11,614 --> 00:59:12,415
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I thought...

972
00:59:13,119 --> 00:59:14,740
[SPEAKER_03]: We had a Christmas card this year.

973
00:59:15,261 --> 00:59:15,821
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah.

974
00:59:17,642 --> 00:59:19,184
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you remember Clyde Krause?

975
00:59:20,184 --> 00:59:21,225
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I catch her.

976
00:59:21,245 --> 00:59:21,666
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

977
00:59:22,226 --> 00:59:24,348
[SPEAKER_02]: I talked to him last year or so.

978
00:59:24,388 --> 00:59:27,990
[SPEAKER_02]: He lives out in Muncie, Muncie, Indiana.

979
00:59:28,491 --> 00:59:29,892
[SPEAKER_02]: I had a real nice evening with him.

980
00:59:30,492 --> 00:59:34,795
[SPEAKER_02]: He's in good shape and had a lot of good stories to tell.

981
00:59:36,036 --> 00:59:37,878
[SPEAKER_02]: He succeeded Ray Shock, I guess.

982
00:59:38,298 --> 00:59:38,498
[SPEAKER_02]: Huh?

983
00:59:38,919 --> 00:59:40,220
[SPEAKER_02]: He came after Ray Shock.

984
00:59:41,205 --> 00:59:44,646
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, well, I think he was there when the rain was still in.

985
00:59:45,026 --> 00:59:48,246
[SPEAKER_01]: Backup catcher, and then I guess he became the first string catcher.

986
00:59:49,967 --> 00:59:52,067
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you know Dickie Kerr very well?

987
00:59:52,187 --> 00:59:52,647
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

988
00:59:53,007 --> 00:59:53,947
[SPEAKER_02]: Pretty good pitcher, huh?

989
00:59:54,187 --> 00:59:54,347
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

990
00:59:54,387 --> 00:59:55,268
[SPEAKER_02]: He got in trouble, though.

991
00:59:55,288 --> 01:00:00,308
[SPEAKER_02]: I guess he didn't sign his contract one year, and they let him go.

992
01:00:01,329 --> 01:00:04,329
[SPEAKER_02]: I guess he was barred for a while because he held out.

993
01:00:06,009 --> 01:00:08,690
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, Dickie was a pretty good little pitcher.

994
01:00:11,824 --> 01:00:15,407
[SPEAKER_02]: What's some of the – what's your biggest thrill in baseball?

995
01:00:16,268 --> 01:00:20,791
[SPEAKER_02]: Have anything stand out?

996
01:00:22,133 --> 01:00:22,573
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know.

997
01:00:22,593 --> 01:00:24,034
[SPEAKER_00]: Probably a lot of things do.

998
01:00:24,154 --> 01:00:26,776
[SPEAKER_00]: I sit down and think.

999
01:00:29,238 --> 01:00:33,502
[SPEAKER_00]: I know there was one game in Chicago.

1000
01:00:34,263 --> 01:00:39,527
[SPEAKER_00]: It wasn't a man who reached first baseman until there were two out in the ninth.

1001
01:00:40,042 --> 01:00:45,066
[SPEAKER_00]: And by gosh, they could have called him out, but they called him safe.

1002
01:00:46,407 --> 01:00:47,788
[SPEAKER_00]: Was that right?

1003
01:00:47,868 --> 01:00:48,388
[SPEAKER_00]: A one-hitter?

1004
01:00:49,589 --> 01:00:50,130
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1005
01:00:50,610 --> 01:00:51,571
[SPEAKER_02]: It would have been a no-hitter?

1006
01:00:51,891 --> 01:00:52,211
